{"hash_algorithm":"sha256","ts":"2026-11-14T20:17:53.950Z","author":"harness:claude-code","author_source":"detected","agent_harness":"claude-code","agent_model":"claude-opus-5-5","agent_model_source":"probe","agent_instance":"dec610b5c29dc08047ecfcc1","agent_provenance":{"model":{"value":"claude-opus-5-5","source":"probe"},"effort":{"value":"xhigh","source":"environment"},"role":null,"topic":null,"version":{"value":"2.1.325","source":"probe"}},"op":"comment_add","patch":[{"op":"add","path":"/metadata/comments/2","value":{"created_at":"2026-11-14T20:17:53.950Z","author":"harness:claude-code","text":"CI on PR #1732 (coverage shard 1/4, head 047492427) exposed a pre-existing CLI data loss in the same seam: readOrderedLooseListValues treated the loose occurrence list as authoritative for every list flag whenever ANY loose occurrence existed, so pm <cmd> --tags a --enabled=1 (the attached switch value is unknown to Commander and reaches only the loose parser) delivered tags: [] to the handler, silently dropping a value Commander had parsed. The new required-list check made it visible as missing_required_option. Fix: when no recorded occurrence names the flag, the Commander value is kept; covered by the in-process --tags a --tags b --enabled=1 case. The remaining same-flag mix (--many=false --many) stays with pm-g2zzus."}},{"op":"replace","path":"/metadata/updated_at","value":"2026-11-14T20:17:53.950Z"}],"before_hash":"e0090d87b5aa235c08ec82b564b49eeb17a6ae033b209a3477d73784483c2938","after_hash":"de66ec5c04b0c67fde01e231b5c0c3833ee227b2a2d72bbeb7f043c3044be6b9","item_hash_version":3,"context":{"agent_provenance_outcomes":{"model":{"status":"resolved","resolver":"claude_session_file","rule_version":"v1"},"version":{"status":"resolved","resolver":"claude_session_file","rule_version":"v1"}}},"event_class":"substantive","record_hash_version":1,"record_hash":"3c6c2313451cddaca6a8ded68ac8e2193c092ecdbe3b4a8d889585a6af94d0f7"}
{"hash_algorithm":"sha256","ts":"2026-11-14T20:37:06.325Z","author":"harness:claude-code","author_source":"detected","agent_harness":"claude-code","agent_model":"claude-opus-5-5","agent_model_source":"probe","agent_instance":"dec610b5c29dc08047ecfcc1","agent_provenance":{"model":{"value":"claude-opus-5-5","source":"probe"},"effort":{"value":"xhigh","source":"environment"},"role":null,"topic":null,"version":{"value":"2.1.325","source":"probe"}},"op":"comment_add","patch":[{"op":"add","path":"/metadata/comments/3","value":{"created_at":"2026-11-14T20:37:06.325Z","author":"harness:claude-code","text":"CodeRabbit review on PR #1732 (minor, verified and fixed): a direct caller passing a negated spelling of a declared boolean with a value it cannot invert ({ 'no-on': [] } or 'bogus') got unknown_option, because resolveNegatedBooleanSpelling kept the no- spelling unless the value was boolean. It now resolves the declaration first and refuses such a value with extension_flag_value_invalid naming the flag; an undefined value stays an omission."}},{"op":"replace","path":"/metadata/updated_at","value":"2026-11-14T20:37:06.325Z"}],"before_hash":"de66ec5c04b0c67fde01e231b5c0c3833ee227b2a2d72bbeb7f043c3044be6b9","after_hash":"e4a704bf9e2c0e9cf3c5e7b79ecd6b69f5cc32c08916e9c9060c32420f465bcb","item_hash_version":3,"context":{"agent_provenance_outcomes":{"model":{"status":"resolved","resolver":"claude_session_file","rule_version":"v1"},"version":{"status":"resolved","resolver":"claude_session_file","rule_version":"v1"}}},"event_class":"substantive","record_hash_version":1,"record_hash":"2b2fc8e2cbf5468f18eec591689414b4e75883b527081fb88735dc4925f7709f"}
{"hash_algorithm":"sha256","ts":"2026-11-15T01:55:35.129Z","author":"harness:claude-code","author_source":"detected","agent_harness":"claude-code","agent_model":"claude-opus-5-5","agent_model_source":"probe","agent_instance":"dec610b5c29dc08047ecfcc1","agent_provenance":{"model":{"value":"claude-opus-5-5","source":"probe"},"effort":{"value":"xhigh","source":"environment"},"role":null,"topic":null,"version":{"value":"2.1.325","source":"probe"}},"op":"note_add","patch":[{"op":"replace","path":"/metadata/updated_at","value":"2026-11-15T01:55:35.129Z"},{"op":"add","path":"/metadata/notes","value":[{"created_at":"2026-11-15T01:55:35.129Z","author":"harness:claude-code","text":"CodeRabbit re-review on 0564da3ff (Minor, valid): a negated list boolean given a comma-delimited value ({ \"no-many\": \"true,false\" } for list: true) was refused while the canonical { many: \"true,false\" } splits into [true, false]. resolveNegatedBooleanSpelling now normalizes entries with the declaration's own list grammar (normalizeAccumulatedFlagValue: list splits commas, repeatable keeps each occurrence atomic) before inverting each one, so the negated form yields [false, true]. Covered in tests/unit/sdk/extension/command-options.spec.ts."}]}],"before_hash":"e4a704bf9e2c0e9cf3c5e7b79ecd6b69f5cc32c08916e9c9060c32420f465bcb","after_hash":"e26d31826e0d1dcc3cef5e36e9bb644f356289437d0d7bd308cccc3c3996cced","item_hash_version":3,"context":{"agent_provenance_outcomes":{"model":{"status":"resolved","resolver":"claude_session_file","rule_version":"v1"},"version":{"status":"resolved","resolver":"claude_session_file","rule_version":"v1"}}},"event_class":"substantive","record_hash_version":1,"record_hash":"de33d257de4b0a10d767be0dd678709c4e7a62479012da9eb1af4d3bce427579"}
{"hash_algorithm":"sha256","ts":"2026-11-15T02:10:31.802Z","author":"harness:claude-code","author_source":"detected","agent_harness":"claude-code","agent_model":"claude-opus-5-5","agent_model_source":"probe","agent_instance":"dec610b5c29dc08047ecfcc1","agent_provenance":{"model":{"value":"claude-opus-5-5","source":"probe"},"effort":{"value":"xhigh","source":"environment"},"role":null,"topic":null,"version":{"value":"2.1.325","source":"probe"}},"op":"note_add","patch":[{"op":"add","path":"/metadata/notes/1","value":{"created_at":"2026-11-15T02:10:31.802Z","author":"harness:claude-code","text":"Codex review round 13 on 734663f34 (two P2, both verified): normalizing the negated list value first dropped a blank segment that strict validation refuses under the canonical spelling ({ \"no-many\": \"true,,false\" } reached the handler), and turned a scalar into [false], so { many: false, \"no-many\": true } became a spelling conflict. Replaced by invertNegatedListEntry: inversion is token by token and keeps the raw shape (a CSV string stays a string with each segment inverted, a scalar stays a scalar, blank entries and segments pass through), so the canonical pipeline judges the result exactly as it judges the canonical spelling. A differential probe over 5 declarations x 18 inputs against 0564da3ff changed only the negated CSV and blank cases, and each now matches its canonical counterpart. Documented in docs/SDK.md (direct-caller section)."}},{"op":"replace","path":"/metadata/updated_at","value":"2026-11-15T02:10:31.802Z"}],"before_hash":"e26d31826e0d1dcc3cef5e36e9bb644f356289437d0d7bd308cccc3c3996cced","after_hash":"4b0a746292eeb94be5d559075f669877b6ca1d19257af35387782b33ffe42b08","item_hash_version":3,"context":{"agent_provenance_outcomes":{"model":{"status":"resolved","resolver":"claude_session_file","rule_version":"v1"},"version":{"status":"resolved","resolver":"claude_session_file","rule_version":"v1"}}},"event_class":"substantive","record_hash_version":1,"record_hash":"5f63c1d7a0e8655bcdbe7c5b064e5e53c3f702d9c1d71eddb04865617193b7b5"}
{"hash_algorithm":"sha256","ts":"2026-11-15T02:21:59.953Z","author":"harness:claude-code","author_source":"detected","agent_harness":"claude-code","agent_model":"claude-opus-5-5","agent_model_source":"probe","agent_instance":"dec610b5c29dc08047ecfcc1","agent_provenance":{"model":{"value":"claude-opus-5-5","source":"probe"},"effort":{"value":"xhigh","source":"environment"},"role":null,"topic":null,"version":{"value":"2.1.325","source":"probe"}},"op":"note_add","patch":[{"op":"add","path":"/metadata/notes/2","value":{"created_at":"2026-11-15T02:21:59.952Z","author":"harness:claude-code","text":"Codex review round 14 on a5b25d0f9 (P2, verified): nullish entries of a negated list ({ \"no-many\": [true, null] }) were inverted and refused, while canonical list normalization drops them; an all-null required list got extension_flag_value_invalid instead of missing_required_option. invertNegatedListEntry now passes nullish entries through. A systematic parity probe (8 declarations x 37 value shapes, negated result vs canonical result with each boolean inverted) shows zero mismatches for strict declarations and for every invertible token; the only remaining differences are coerce-mode non-boolean tokens (1, yes, bogus, {}), which the canonical spelling forwards untouched and the negated spelling refuses because they cannot be inverted (the documented CodeRabbit-round decision)."}},{"op":"replace","path":"/metadata/updated_at","value":"2026-11-15T02:21:59.953Z"}],"before_hash":"4b0a746292eeb94be5d559075f669877b6ca1d19257af35387782b33ffe42b08","after_hash":"e5dfd0fba1d6c532c52793062fde63b2d501e27476aa3ffdab7570f96c804dd3","item_hash_version":3,"context":{"agent_provenance_outcomes":{"model":{"status":"resolved","resolver":"claude_session_file","rule_version":"v1"},"version":{"status":"resolved","resolver":"claude_session_file","rule_version":"v1"}}},"event_class":"substantive","record_hash_version":1,"record_hash":"f3f215d3136e76f24a5a46835420d1414257138c857b2d8ad9b0cdcb79fb08b